<p>&#8220;One of humanity’s oldest disciplines and one of its newest inventions feel distinctly made for each other.&#8221;<span id="more-57756"></span></p>
<p><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" class="wp-image-57757 aligncenter" src="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/nyt-philosophy-majors-ai.png" alt="" width="650" height="403" srcset="https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/nyt-philosophy-majors-ai.png 966w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/nyt-philosophy-majors-ai-300x186.png 300w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/nyt-philosophy-majors-ai-768x476.png 768w, https://dailynous.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/nyt-philosophy-majors-ai-400x248.png 400w" sizes="(max-width: 650px) 100vw, 650px" /></p>
<p>That&#8217;s a line from an article published in the <a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/07/05/business/philosophy-majors-ai-jobs.html?unlocked_article_code=1.vVA.udfZ.UjuOTMOAYIic&amp;smid=url-share" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><em>New York Times</em></a> today about the demand for philosophers working on artificial intelligence.</p>
<p>It continues:</p>
<p style="padding-left: 40px;"><em>A.I. presents a fresh way for philosophers to ask ancient questions, and its own set of new ones that they are uniquely trained to engage with: of truth and belief and knowledge (epistemologists); of reasoning (logicians); of mind and consciousness (philosophers of mind and consciousness). For ethicists, in particular, A.I. is a bonanza. How should models act toward us? How should humans interact with them? Where would purpose come from in a post-work society?</em></p>
<p>The article discusses several people trained in philosophy who are working for AI firms or AI-related organizations, including Robert Long, Geoff Keeling, Iason Gabriel, Patrick Butlin, and Amanda Askell. (See &#8220;<a href="https://dailynous.com/2026/03/17/philosophers-working-in-or-with-ai-firms-organizations/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Philosophers Working in or with AI Firms &amp; Organizations</a>&#8220;.)</p>
<p>It also covers some of the topics of interest to these philosophers, such as the nature of consciousness and AI welfare.</p>
<p>Unfortunately, in passing, the article says: &#8220;a plain-vanilla philosophy degree remains as hard to monetize as ever.&#8221; The implication is that philosophy majors in general fare poorly on the job market. However, as we have seen time and time again (most recently, see some of the data <a href="https://dailynous.com/2026/07/01/dept-of-education-earnings-premium-rule-philosophy-ok/">here</a>) that is just not true.</p>
